<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rigveda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rig Veda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rigveda is the oldest of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the sacred texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>: a collection of 1,028 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> hymns arranged in ten books. Composed in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> roughly between 1500 and 1000 BCE and handed down orally with great precision, it praises gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Agni and Soma and names tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatas">Bharatas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purus">Purus</a>. It is the earliest major source for Vedic religion and the Indo-Aryan languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rigveda.jpg" alt="Rigveda" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rigveda_MS2097.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Rigveda is the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> text, and its early layers are among the oldest extant texts in any Indo-European language. Philological evidence indicates that the bulk of the Rigveda Samhita was composed in the northwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, most likely between c. 1500 and 1000 BCE, although a wider estimate of c. 1900 to 1200 BCE has also been given. All proposals are inferred from the style and content of the hymns, and the scholars Jamison and Brereton wrote in 2014 that the dating is likely to remain a matter of contention.</p><p>According to Witzel, the hymns were first collected after the Bharata victory under King Sudās in the Battle of the Ten Kings, and the codification took place about 1200 BCE among the early Kuru tribe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Rigveda Samhita is a collection of ten books, called maṇḍalas, with 1,028 hymns in about 10,600 verses. Books 2 to 7, the family books, are the oldest and shortest, each associated with a clan, and they mention prominent Bharata and Pūru kings. The ninth book is entirely dedicated to Soma and the Soma ritual. Books 1 and 10 in part deal with philosophical questions such as the origin of the universe.</p><p>The hymns are addressed to many deities. Chief among them are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, a heroic god praised for slaying his enemy Vrtra, Agni, the sacrificial fire, and Soma, the sacred potion or the plant it is made from. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> and Varuna and Ushas, the dawn, are equally prominent. Tradition associates a rishi, or composer, with each verse, and ten families of rishis account for more than 95 per cent of the verses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The hymns were composed and preserved orally, and memorised and handed on with what is called unmatched fidelity through structured methods of enunciation. According to Barbara West the text was probably first written down about the 3rd century BCE. Thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, collected in the 19th century and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, were added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register in 2007.</p><p>The Rigveda's archaic syntax and morphology are of vital importance for reconstructing Proto-Indo-European, and some verses are still recited at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> weddings and other rites of passage, which makes it probably the world's oldest religious text in continued use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rigveda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The hymns of the <b>Rigveda</b>, composed in an early form of Sanskrit perhaps between 1500 and 1200 BCE, praise gods of nature and order such as Indra, Agni and Varuna and accompany sacrifices performed by priests around a sacred fire.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Mithras takes his name from Mithra, an old pre-Zoroastrian and later Zoroastrian god of Iran; the related Mitra is praised in the <b>Rigveda</b>, and the form mi-it-ra- appears in a treaty between the Hittites and Mitanni of about 1400 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The language of the hymns attributed to him, the Gathas, is an archaic form of Avestan, an Iranian language closely related to the Sanskrit of the <b>Rigveda</b>, and the world they describe is one of herders and their cattle without cities or kings.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The oldest of them, the <b>Rigveda</b>, a collection of 1,028 hymns to the gods in ten books, was composed in the region of the Punjab around 1500 to 1200 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Most scholars date the <b>Rigveda</b>, the oldest text of the Vedic tradition, to the later 2nd millennium BCE, after the cities had gone, and how far the Harappans’ descendants and traditions shaped later Indian culture is still debated.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy combined pastoralism and agriculture: the <b>Rigveda</b> mentions levelling fields, processing seed and storing grain in large jars, and exchange ran through gifts to kings (bali) and priests (dana) and barter with cattle as a unit of value.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Society</a></li></ul></section>
